1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,860
Google just launched an AI into space.

2
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:06,580
And the very first thing
it said back… was a poem.

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:10,130
Okay, to be fair… they asked for one.

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:14,050
But that little poem is the
start of a really big idea.

5
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:17,630
A month ago, a SpaceX rocket
carried a fridge-sized satellite

6
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:20,550
into orbit, built with a
company called Planet.

7
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:25,660
Inside: a Google AI chip, the kind
it uses in its giant data centers,

8
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:28,190
running Google's own AI model, Gemma.

9
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:30,510
It circles Earth about
every hour and a half,

10
00:00:30,510 --> 00:00:33,380
so engineers message it
each time it flies over.

11
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:34,640
Their first request?

12
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,550
A haiku about space.

13
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:38,770
It came down over the top of the world:

14
00:00:38,770 --> 00:00:41,400
3 short lines about looking down at Earth.

15
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,500
But why go to all this trouble?

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:45,810
Because AI is hungry.

17
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:49,290
Almost every AI answer you
get comes from a data center:

18
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:52,560
a building full of computers,
all eating electricity.

19
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,340
By 2030, data centers could use
more electricity than the whole

20
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:58,100
of Japan uses today.

21
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,880
So Google looked up.

22
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:03,470
The sun puts out a hundred trillion
times more energy than all

23
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:05,180
the electricity people make.

24
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,460
Down here, solar panels stop at night,

25
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,020
and clouds get in the way.

26
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:12,520
But in the right orbit, riding
the line where day meets night,

27
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:14,620
the sun almost never sets.

28
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:17,670
A panel up there makes up
to eight times more power.

29
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:19,450
Free sunshine, forever.

30
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:20,390
Perfect?

31
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:21,920
Not quite.

32
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,280
Chips turn electricity into heat.

33
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:27,010
You know when your phone gets
too hot and has to cool down?

34
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:30,070
On Earth, fans and water
carry that heat away.

35
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:31,110
In space?

36
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:32,010
No air.

37
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:32,860
No wind.

38
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:33,830
No water.

39
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:35,080
Think of a thermos.

40
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,060
The empty gap between its
walls keeps your coffee hot.

41
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:40,820
Space… is one giant thermos.

42
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:44,140
Up there, heat can only
escape as invisible light,

43
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:45,670
like the warmth of a fire.

44
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:48,460
So the satellite carries
panels, called radiators,

45
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:49,700
to glow it away.

46
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:54,080
For now, the AI works in short
bursts… then rests to cool down.

47
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:55,740
Then there's radiation.

48
00:01:55,740 --> 00:01:59,220
Tiny, fast particles can
flip a one into a zero.

49
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:01,940
A typo, right in the middle
of the AI's thinking.

50
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:05,250
So Google blasted its chips with
a particle beam while they ran

51
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:05,990
AI.

52
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:09,100
They survived more than five
years' worth of radiation.

53
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:10,080
The big dream?

54
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,330
Dozens of satellites, flying
a hundred or so meters apart,

55
00:02:13,330 --> 00:02:15,780
sharing the work and talking by laser.

56
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:19,810
Google says aiming those lasers
is like hitting a coin from miles

57
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:22,300
away… while you're both moving.

58
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,200
Two test satellites go up in 2027.

59
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:26,800
But let's be honest.

60
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:30,290
Google's own math says launches
need to get much cheaper:

61
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:32,540
under two hundred dollars a kilo.

62
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,730
No one can fix a broken chip up there.

63
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:38,780
And even the project's leader says it
won't be cheaper than Earth for years.

64
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:41,420
So that little poem
tells us something big.

65
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:45,630
Every question you ask an AI costs
power… and the search for more

66
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:46,900
just left the planet.

67
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,090
There's one word in this
video I never said out loud.

68
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:50,930
Spotted it?

69
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:51,710
Comment it.

70
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:54,590
Follow for more AI, explained like this.
